        explanation: "これ is near me, それ is near you, あれ is over there away from both of us. These three stand alone: これは ほんです = 'this is a book'. When you want to attach the pointer to a noun, use この・その・あの instead, and they always need a noun right after: この ほん = 'this book'. Ask what something is with これは なんですか. Agree with そうです ('that's right'); show mild surprise with そうですか ('oh, really?').",
        watchFor: "これ・それ・あれ stand alone; この・その・あの can never stand alone — always この＋noun.",
        commonError: "Saying この on its own for 'this one', or これ ほん with no は. Use これは for 'this is…', and この ほん for 'this book'.",
